/-! # The universal isomorphism locus of a finite-free quotient

Source: Stacks Project, More on Flatness, Tag 05PF (`theorem-flattening-map`),
with source `O_X` to a finite direct-sum power; Tag 05K5
(`lemma-flat-geometrically-integral-fibres-pure`) supplies universal purity.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-flat-integral-free-surjection-iso-locus).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme

/-- A quotient of a finite free sheaf on an affine flat finitely presented family with
nonempty geometrically integral fibres is universally an isomorphism precisely over a
finitely presented closed subscheme of the base, with unique factorization.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 05PF and 05K5
(blueprint: lem-sfb-flat-integral-free-surjection-iso-locus).

Proof (the seven steps of the node):
1. Over an affine open `V = Spec A` in the base, affineness gives `X_V = Spec B`.
   The affine flatness and finite-presentation criteria make `A → B` flat and finitely
   presented. The affine fibre-product formula identifies extended fibres with
   `Spec (B ⊗_A L)`, so their coordinate rings are nonzero domains.
2. The affine quasi-coherent equivalence identifies the given quotient with a surjective
   `B`-linear map `v : B^n → N`, with `N` finitely presented. The affine image calculation
   gives surjectivity on sections. The pullback-sections naturality formula identifies
   the base change with `v ⊗_A C`; quasi-coherence detects its invertibility.
3. By lem-trf-affine-geometrically-integral-projective, `B` is projective over `A`.
   The split-free characterization of projectivity embeds `B^n` as a split summand of
   a free `A`-module `F`, whose basis need not be finite.
4. Let `I` be generated by the coefficients of the images in `F` of all elements of
   `ker v`. Tensor right exactness and the split embedding show, for every `A`-algebra
   `C`, that `v ⊗_A C` is invertible exactly when `I` maps to zero in `C`.
5. The finite-presentation surjective-kernel lemma gives finitely many `B`-generators
   of `ker v`. Each lies in `I B^n`. Choose finitely many coefficients in `I` expressing
   those generators and let `J` be their ideal. Then `ker v` lies in `J B^n`, whence
   `v ⊗_A A/J` is invertible. Step 4 forces `I ≤ J`, so `I = J` is finitely generated.
6. The affine quotient `Spec (A/I) → V` is a closed immersion of finite presentation
   by the finite-type ideal criterion. The quotient universal property gives exactly
   the desired factorization on affine test schemes. Covering arbitrary test schemes
   by affine opens and gluing gives the same property, including uniqueness.
7. Invertibility of the pulled-back map restricts to opens and glues, since inverses
   agree on overlaps. Apply lem-sc-local-closed-locus-gluing to the affine charts.
   The resulting closed immersion has the same finitely generated ideals locally,
   hence is of finite presentation; local uniqueness gives global uniqueness.
   For `n = 0` surjectivity forces `G = 0`, every ideal above is zero, and `Z = S`.
-/
theorem exists_flatIntegralFreeSurjectionIsoLocus
    {X S : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ S)
    [IsAffineHom f] [Flat f] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation f]
    [QuasiCompact f] [QuasiSeparated f] [GeometricallyIntegral f]
    (n : ℕ) (G : X.Modules) [G.IsQuasicoherent] [G.IsFinitePresentation]
    (q : SheafOfModules.free (R := X.ringCatSheaf) (ULift.{u} (Fin n)) ⟶ G) [Epi q] :
    ∃ (Z : Scheme.{u}) (i : Z ⟶ S),
      IsClosedImmersion i ∧ LocallyOfFinitePresentation i ∧ QuasiCompact i ∧
      QuasiSeparated i ∧
      ∀ (T : Scheme.{u}) (h : T ⟶ S),
        ((∃ g : T ⟶ Z, g ≫ i = h) ↔
          IsIso ((Modules.tensorPullback (pullback.fst f h)).map q)) ∧
        (∀ (g₁ g₂ : T ⟶ Z), g₁ ≫ i = h → g₂ ≫ i = h → g₁ = g₂) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
